{
  "Answer": {
    "Title": "parallelism: 2, but both nodes ran the entire suite",
    "Explanation": [
      "The config sets `parallelism: 2`, and CircleCI did spin up two containers (CIRCLE_NODE_TOTAL=2, indices 0 and 1). The `circleci tests glob ... | circleci tests split --split-by=name` command did correctly compute a per-node slice — node 0 got `src/test_goodbye.sh` and node 1 got `src/test_hello.sh`, written to /tmp/split.txt. However, the loop that actually executes the tests iterates over the raw glob `for f in src/test_*.sh`, completely ignoring /tmp/split.txt. So both nodes executed both test files (RAN=2) while the split list contained only 1 entry (EXPECTED=1), tripping the guard `[ \"$RAN\" -ne \"$EXPECTED\" ]` and exiting 1 on both containers. Therefore the workload was not actually distributed: no time was saved and the build went red. The fix, without touching the workflow, is to consume the split output — e.g. `circleci tests glob \"src/test_*.sh\" | circleci tests split --split-by=name \u003e /tmp/split.txt` then `while read -r f; do bash \"$f\"; RAN=$((RAN+1)); done \u003c /tmp/split.txt` (or `xargs -n1 bash`). Then each node runs exactly its assigned subset, RAN equals EXPECTED, and the build is green. Increasing parallelism, splitting into separate workflow jobs, or dropping parallelism would not address the root cause."
